    "response": "To reduce the number of bins used in online bin packing, we can modify the score function to prioritize bins with a lower score and a higher index, which allows us to potentially fill more bins before filling bins with higher scores. Here's the modified function:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef score(item, bins):\n    feasible_mask = bins - item >= 0\n    feasible_bins = bins[feasible_mask]\n    sorted_indices = np.argsort(feasible_bins)\n    sorted_feasible_bins = feasible_bins[sorted_indices]\n    \n    # Calculate the score for each feasible bin\n    scores = np.zeros(len(sorted_feasible_bins))\n    for i, bin in enumerate(sorted_feasible_bins):\n        score = (bin - item) ** 2\n        scores[i] = score\n    \n    # Return the scores in reverse order to prioritize lower scores first\n    return scores[::-1]\n```\n\nThis function first identifies the feasible bins and sorts them based on their capacity. It then calculates the score for each feasible bin and returns the scores in reverse order, which prioritizes lower scores first. This approach allows us to potentially fill more bins before filling bins with higher scores.",
